WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.332
<v Examiner>The ear and sound, the fine print.

2
00:00:03.332 --> 00:00:05.762
<v Examiner>Her worksheets, her tables, and her orders.

3
00:00:06.212 --> 00:00:09.853
The written tracks walk you through the ear and through waves.

4
00:00:09.853 --> 00:00:13.787
Her worksheets and matching tables ask for more of the small parts,

5
00:00:13.787 --> 00:00:15.960
and for things in a particular order.

6
00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:17.135
This track is those.

7
00:00:17.585 --> 00:00:19.478
The hook: air, bone, fluid.

8
00:00:19.478 --> 00:00:22.773
The oval window pushes, the round window gives.

9
00:00:22.773 --> 00:00:28.102
Low potassium outside, high potassium inside, and glutamate fires the nerve.

10
00:00:28.552 --> 00:00:29.413
The outer ear.

11
00:00:29.413 --> 00:00:33.350
The pinna is made of cartilage, with the ear lobe at the bottom.

12
00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:37.103
It picks up sound waves and channels them down the ear canal,

13
00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:40.486
which her worksheet calls the external auditory meatus.

14
00:00:40.486 --> 00:00:44.669
The ceruminous glands in the canal make cerumen, a yellow-brown wax.

15
00:00:44.669 --> 00:00:49.160
If it packs the canal, the person struggles to hear: conduction deafness.

16
00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:50.554
The middle ear.

17
00:00:50.554 --> 00:00:53.574
The middle ear cavity sits in the temporal bone.

18
00:00:53.574 --> 00:00:57.224
The tympanic membrane, the eardrum, is a relatively large,

19
00:00:57.224 --> 00:01:01.503
tough membrane that turns vibrations in air into vibrations in bone.

20
00:01:01.503 --> 00:01:04.901
The ossicles act as levers, to amplify the vibrations,

21
00:01:04.901 --> 00:01:08.299
and the stapes is the smallest bone in the human body.

22
00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:11.948
The Eustachian tube connects the middle ear to the throat,

23
00:01:11.948 --> 00:01:14.591
and equalises the pressure on the eardrum.

24
00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:19.855
Her true or false: in the outer ear, sound travels through air; in the middle ear,

25
00:01:19.855 --> 00:01:22.791
through bone; and in the inner ear, through fluid.

26
00:01:22.791 --> 00:01:23.084
True.

27
00:01:23.084 --> 00:01:27.253
The oval window is where vibrations in bone become vibrations in fluid.

28
00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:32.537
And the round window: the oval window and the round window vibrate in opposite directions.

29
00:01:32.537 --> 00:01:36.587
As the stapes pushes the oval window in, the round window bulges out,

30
00:01:36.587 --> 00:01:38.349
so the fluid has room to move.

31
00:01:38.349 --> 00:01:43.574
Her order of the membranes: the tympanic membrane, the oval window, the basilar membrane,

32
00:01:43.574 --> 00:01:44.865
then the round window.

33
00:01:45.315 --> 00:01:46.119
The inner ear.

34
00:01:46.119 --> 00:01:48.586
The cochlea is a fluid-filled, coiled tube,

35
00:01:48.586 --> 00:01:51.513
lined with the sensory cells of the organ of Corti,

36
00:01:51.513 --> 00:01:54.726
which turn vibrations in fluid into electrical impulses.

37
00:01:54.726 --> 00:01:59.317
The semicircular canals are fluid-filled, and set at right angles to each other,

38
00:01:59.317 --> 00:02:01.842
so they pick up movement in every direction.

39
00:02:01.842 --> 00:02:06.662
Two nerves leave the inner ear: the vestibular nerve, for balance, and the cochlear,

40
00:02:06.662 --> 00:02:08.498
or auditory, nerve, for hearing,

41
00:02:08.498 --> 00:02:12.515
which carries the impulses as action potentials to the temporal lobes.

42
00:02:13.965 --> 00:02:15.522
Her organ of Corti sheet.

43
00:02:15.522 --> 00:02:20.816
Perilymph fills the scala vestibuli and the scala tympani, and it's low in potassium.

44
00:02:20.816 --> 00:02:24.553
Endolymph fills the scala media, and it's high in potassium.

45
00:02:24.553 --> 00:02:29.412
When the hairs bend, potassium and calcium flow into the top of the hair cell,

46
00:02:29.412 --> 00:02:32.339
and it releases the neurotransmitter glutamate,

47
00:02:32.339 --> 00:02:35.391
which sends an impulse on to the auditory cortex.

48
00:02:35.391 --> 00:02:39.813
Her other wording: the stereocilia bend against the tectorial membrane,

49
00:02:39.813 --> 00:02:44.795
excitatory neurotransmitter is released at the synapse with the sensory neurons,

50
00:02:44.795 --> 00:02:48.470
and action potentials travel to the central nervous system.

51
00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.339
Mechanical in, electrical out.

52
00:02:50.789 --> 00:02:51.428
Her orders.

53
00:02:51.428 --> 00:02:54.743
Her worksheet's seven steps: movement of the oval window,

54
00:02:54.743 --> 00:02:59.453
movement of the perilymph in the scala tympani, movement of the basilar membrane,

55
00:02:59.453 --> 00:03:02.069
movement of the endolymph in the scala media,

56
00:03:02.069 --> 00:03:06.140
bending of the sensory hairs as the gel-like tectorial membrane moves,

57
00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:10.792
impulses along the cochlear nerve, and the auditory cortex in the temporal lobe.

58
00:03:10.792 --> 00:03:14.804
Notice that her orders put the basilar membrane before the endolymph.

59
00:03:14.804 --> 00:03:17.711
In her arrange-the-steps questions, use her order.

60
00:03:18.161 --> 00:03:19.672
Pitch along the cochlea.

61
00:03:19.672 --> 00:03:24.583
Low sounds move the longer, floppier fibres, at the apex of the cochlear duct,

62
00:03:24.583 --> 00:03:26.472
furthest from the oval window.

63
00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:30.878
High-pitched sounds move the shorter, stiffer hair cells, at the base.

64
00:03:30.878 --> 00:03:33.774
Her receptors table says the same: high pitch,

65
00:03:33.774 --> 00:03:38.748
hair cells at the base of the cochlear duct; low pitch, hair cells at the apex.

66
00:03:40.198 --> 00:03:43.513
Her light-and-sound table, ten marks, in two quizzes.

67
00:03:43.513 --> 00:03:47.140
Sound waves are mechanical waves: vibrations of particles,

68
00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:49.517
so they can't travel through a vacuum.

69
00:03:49.517 --> 00:03:52.707
Light waves are electromagnetic waves, so they can.

70
00:03:52.707 --> 00:03:55.834
Photons are light particles, or packages of light.

71
00:03:55.834 --> 00:04:00.712
The amplitude of light gives its intensity, how bright it is, measured in lux.

72
00:04:00.712 --> 00:04:04.715
The amplitude of sound gives its loudness, measured in decibels.

73
00:04:04.715 --> 00:04:10.344
The frequency and wavelength of light give its colour: red light has the lowest frequency,

74
00:04:10.344 --> 00:04:11.782
and violet the highest.

75
00:04:11.782 --> 00:04:15.410
Of sound, its pitch: a higher frequency is a higher pitch.

76
00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:18.224
And all light waves travel at the same speed,

77
00:04:18.224 --> 00:04:21.414
about three hundred thousand kilometres per second.

78
00:04:21.864 --> 00:04:23.744
Her wave table's definitions.

79
00:04:23.744 --> 00:04:27.439
Wavelength: the distance between the crests of the waves,

80
00:04:27.439 --> 00:04:29.579
measured in nanometres for light.

81
00:04:29.579 --> 00:04:33.144
Frequency: how often a wave passes a point each second.

82
00:04:33.144 --> 00:04:38.525
Amplitude: the maximum displacement, or height, of the wave from its resting point.

83
00:04:38.975 --> 00:04:40.820
Two more refraction wordings.

84
00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:46.353
A wave bends because one part of it slows down, or speeds up, relative to another part.

85
00:04:46.353 --> 00:04:50.105
Refraction goes into the new medium, but changes direction;

86
00:04:50.105 --> 00:04:52.522
reflection returns to the same medium.

87
00:04:52.522 --> 00:04:58.055
Her example of refraction: an object in water looks as if it's in a different position,

88
00:04:58.055 --> 00:04:59.899
like a straw that looks bent.

89
00:04:59.899 --> 00:05:02.570
Her example of reflection: a mirror image.

90
00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:04.921
And three of her sense words.

91
00:05:04.921 --> 00:05:08.919
Static and dynamic equilibrium both work by mechanoreception.

92
00:05:08.919 --> 00:05:14.753
Taste and smell are both chemoreception, and her key says taste is eighty per cent smell.

93
00:05:15.203 --> 00:05:15.888
The traps.

94
00:05:15.888 --> 00:05:19.926
The round window moves the opposite way to the oval window.

95
00:05:19.926 --> 00:05:23.280
Endolymph is high in potassium, perilymph is low.

96
00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.634
Low pitch is at the apex, high pitch at the base.

97
00:05:26.634 --> 00:05:32.178
And loud sound is amplitude, measured in decibels; bright light is amplitude too,

98
00:05:32.178 --> 00:05:33.273
measured in lux.

99
00:05:33.723 --> 00:05:34.293
<v Examiner>Once more.

100
00:05:34.293 --> 00:05:36.005
<v Examiner>The ear and sound, fine print.

101
00:05:38.455 --> 00:05:40.988
The pinna is cartilage, with the ear lobe,

102
00:05:40.988 --> 00:05:44.184
and channels sound down the external auditory meatus,

103
00:05:44.184 --> 00:05:48.284
where ceruminous glands make wax that can cause conduction deafness.

104
00:05:48.284 --> 00:05:53.470
The eardrum turns air vibrations into bone vibrations, and the ossicles act as levers;

105
00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:56.123
the stapes is the smallest bone in the body.

106
00:05:56.123 --> 00:05:59.801
Air in the outer ear, bone in the middle, fluid in the inner.

107
00:05:59.801 --> 00:06:03.419
The oval window turns bone vibrations into fluid vibrations,

108
00:06:03.419 --> 00:06:06.073
and the round window moves the opposite way.

109
00:06:06.073 --> 00:06:08.726
The semicircular canals sit at right angles.

110
00:06:08.726 --> 00:06:12.525
The vestibular nerve is balance; the cochlear nerve is hearing.

111
00:06:12.975 --> 00:06:17.209
Perilymph, in the scala vestibuli and tympani, is low in potassium.

112
00:06:17.209 --> 00:06:19.674
Endolymph, in the scala media, is high.

113
00:06:19.674 --> 00:06:24.288
Potassium and calcium flow into the hair cell, and it releases glutamate.

114
00:06:24.288 --> 00:06:27.385
Low pitch is at the apex, high pitch at the base.

115
00:06:27.385 --> 00:06:32.947
Sound is mechanical and can't cross a vacuum; light is electromagnetic, made of photons.

116
00:06:32.947 --> 00:06:37.814
Light's amplitude is its intensity, in lux; sound's is loudness, in decibels.

117
00:06:37.814 --> 00:06:41.290
Red light has the lowest frequency, violet the highest.

118
00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:45.651
Refraction bends a wave as part of it slows, like an object in water.

119
00:06:45.651 --> 00:06:49.696
Taste and smell are chemoreception, balance is mechanoreception,

120
00:06:49.696 --> 00:06:51.908
and taste is eighty per cent smell.
